<title>VENEZUELA: Venezuela stops regional sugar imports.</title>
<headline>Venezuela stops regional sugar imports.</headline>
<dateline>CARACAS 1996-09-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Venezuela has temporarily suspended sugar imports from the Andean Community and Central America due to high inventories at home, government and private sector officials said.</p>
<p>According to a resolution published in Friday's Official Gazette -- where all legislation and decrees are recorded -- the restriction applies to cane sugar, ground and refined sugar from Colombia, Bolivia, Ecuador, Nicaragua, El Salvador and Guatemala.</p>
<p>Venezuela suspended the imports under trade agreements which allow for such temporary measures where national production is affected.</p>
<p>The resolution did not say for how long the sugar imports would be suspended.</p>
<p>According to sugar producers current stocks are high due to a sharp domestic recession.</p>
<p>Figures on sugar imports were not immediately available but the private Union of Venezuelan Sugar Producers (UPAVE) forecasts a 540,000 metric tonne production for the 1996-7 harvest.</p>
<p>--Omar Lugo, Caracas newsroom, 582 505 2600</p>
